1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,746
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,766 --> 00:00:08,745
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:09,145 --> 00:00:14,005
This is Lecture 28, garment processes and the sewing floor.

4
00:00:14,405 --> 00:00:18,569
For more than twenty lectures we've studied one machine.

5
00:00:18,589 --> 00:00:24,097
Today we step onto the floor and see how dozens of machines and people are

6
00:00:24,117 --> 00:00:25,665
arranged into a line.

7
00:00:27,185 --> 00:00:28,645
Here's the problem.

8
00:00:29,045 --> 00:00:35,918
A chinos line has 26 people and a plan of 120 an hour;

9
00:00:35,938 --> 00:00:40,258
23 operations total 13.20 minutes.

10
00:00:40,658 --> 00:00:42,792
Work was split by feel,

11
00:00:42,812 --> 00:00:48,129
roughly one operation per station with extras where busy;

12
00:00:48,149 --> 00:00:54,029
zip setting and inseam take 0.70 minutes each, one person each.

13
00:00:54,429 --> 00:00:56,988
The manager wants more people;

14
00:00:57,008 --> 00:01:03,437
the alternative is an automatic pocket welter and a waistband machine for a

15
00:01:03,457 --> 00:01:05,329
net 200 thousand yuan.

16
00:01:05,729 --> 00:01:12,844
What does the line make now, what could regrouping do, and do the machines pay?

17
00:01:13,244 --> 00:01:14,538
We'll answer at the end.

18
00:01:16,058 --> 00:01:18,198
From sample to bulk production.

19
00:01:19,718 --> 00:01:22,996
A garment takes two journeys through a factory.

20
00:01:23,396 --> 00:01:27,050
First, technical preparation: patterns, sample,

21
00:01:27,070 --> 00:01:29,865
tech pack and pre-production sample,

22
00:01:29,885 --> 00:01:34,244
while the industrial engineer splits it into operations,

23
00:01:34,264 --> 00:01:39,091
estimates times and lays out the line, which is most of today.

24
00:01:39,491 --> 00:01:46,765
Second, bulk production: cutting, sewing, finishing, inspection and packing.

25
00:01:47,165 --> 00:01:50,592
Sewing has the most operations and people,

26
00:01:50,612 --> 00:01:55,025
about 80 percent of labour time in our chinos example.

27
00:01:56,545 --> 00:01:59,245
Operations and standard minutes.

28
00:02:00,765 --> 00:02:03,426
The industrial engineer, or IE,

29
00:02:03,446 --> 00:02:08,703
first splits the garment into operations in processing order,

30
00:02:08,723 --> 00:02:15,190
finely for a long line, coarsely for a cell, each with its machine, stitch,

31
00:02:15,210 --> 00:02:17,785
requirements and predecessors.

32
00:02:18,185 --> 00:02:25,867
The chinos take 23: back, front and fly made separately, then rise, side seams,

33
00:02:25,888 --> 00:02:30,645
inseam, waistband and hems, 13.20 minutes in all.

34
00:02:31,045 --> 00:02:34,928
Times for similar products differ several-fold,

35
00:02:34,948 --> 00:02:37,585
so factories use their own data.

36
00:02:39,105 --> 00:02:44,954
A standard minute is the time a trained worker needs by the set method at

37
00:02:44,974 --> 00:02:45,919
normal pace,

38
00:02:45,939 --> 00:02:47,205
plus allowances.

39
00:02:47,605 --> 00:02:54,456
For the side-seam topstitch: ten stopwatch timings average 0.48 minutes;

40
00:02:54,476 --> 00:03:01,041
the operator is brisk, rated 110 percent, so the basic time is 0.528;

41
00:03:01,061 --> 00:03:06,289
add 14 percent for personal needs, fatigue and machine,

42
00:03:06,309 --> 00:03:11,347
and the standard minute is about 0.60, or 36 seconds.

43
00:03:12,867 --> 00:03:18,462
Operators spend about 80 percent of their time handling fabric.

44
00:03:18,862 --> 00:03:21,325
So how much does top speed buy?

45
00:03:21,725 --> 00:03:26,664
The lab computes one seam with the speed profile,

46
00:03:26,684 --> 00:03:31,320
cycling top speed through 4000, 5000 and 6000.

47
00:03:31,720 --> 00:03:42,207
A 333-stitch seam goes from 5.75 to 4.80 seconds at 5000 instead of 4000;

48
00:03:42,227 --> 00:03:48,540
two seams save only 5 percent of 36 seconds.

49
00:03:48,940 --> 00:03:54,277
A 40-stitch seam saves a tenth of a second at 6000.

50
00:03:54,677 --> 00:03:59,174
Faster ramps, trimming and handling matter more.

51
00:04:00,694 --> 00:04:01,820
Line balancing.

52
00:04:03,340 --> 00:04:08,409
The basic quantities: takt is 60 over target output,

53
00:04:08,429 --> 00:04:11,150
0.5 minutes for 120 an hour;

54
00:04:11,170 --> 00:04:18,001
the theoretical minimum headcount is total time over takt, rounded up,

55
00:04:18,021 --> 00:04:19,176
at least 27.

56
00:04:19,576 --> 00:04:25,635
The busiest person's load is the cycle, output is 60 over the cycle,

57
00:04:25,655 --> 00:04:30,104
and balance is total time over people times cycle.

58
00:04:30,504 --> 00:04:34,524
The busiest person sets the pace of the whole line.

59
00:04:36,044 --> 00:04:40,984
The chart switches between two splits; the red line is takt.

60
00:04:41,384 --> 00:04:48,088
With one operation per station you add people wherever time exceeds takt,

61
00:04:48,108 --> 00:04:54,168
but only in whole people: the 0.55-minute fly topstitch needs two,

62
00:04:54,188 --> 00:05:00,524
each idle half the time, and a 0.2-minute buttonhole still takes one.

63
00:05:00,924 --> 00:05:06,424
Result: 38 people, 69.5 percent balance.

64
00:05:06,824 --> 00:05:14,844
Switch to automatic grouping and the bars even out: 29 people, 91.0 percent.

65
00:05:16,364 --> 00:05:22,064
The answer is to merge neighbouring operations into groups shared by several people.

66
00:05:22,464 --> 00:05:30,225
Front overlocking, back overlocking and back darts total 1.30 minutes;

67
00:05:30,245 --> 00:05:35,004
three people at 0.433 each, all under takt.

68
00:05:35,404 --> 00:05:42,036
Rules: at most four people, four operations and two machine types per group,

69
00:05:42,056 --> 00:05:44,224
and no broken precedence.

70
00:05:44,624 --> 00:05:49,987
Positional weight is an operation's time plus everything after it;

71
00:05:50,007 --> 00:05:55,696
sort by weight and cut with dynamic programming for the fewest people.

72
00:05:57,216 --> 00:06:07,745
Automatic grouping gives 29 people at 91.0 percent for 120 an hour,

73
00:06:07,765 --> 00:06:14,516
36 at 91.7 for 150, and 25 at 91.8 for 100.

74
00:06:14,916 --> 00:06:19,069
Change takt and the rounding changes, so regroup;

75
00:06:19,089 --> 00:06:22,816
a new style or method means rebalancing too.

76
00:06:23,216 --> 00:06:28,815
Theoretical balance ignores individual speed and variation;

77
00:06:28,835 --> 00:06:35,196
next lecture's hanger systems and U cells let the line absorb them.

78
00:06:36,716 --> 00:06:37,872
Lean production.

79
00:06:39,392 --> 00:06:43,913
Lean production grew out of the Japanese car industry,

80
00:06:43,933 --> 00:06:49,126
and its core is removing waste: everything that adds no value.

81
00:06:49,526 --> 00:06:56,300
Garment floors name seven: overproduction, waiting, transport, over-processing,

82
00:06:56,320 --> 00:06:59,826
inventory, unnecessary motion and rework.

83
00:07:00,226 --> 00:07:05,499
A sewing machine adds value only in the seconds it sews;

84
00:07:05,519 --> 00:07:07,956
the rest can all be waste.

85
00:07:09,476 --> 00:07:12,136
Equipment and multi-machine tending.

86
00:07:13,656 --> 00:07:16,195
With groups settled, equip them:

87
00:07:16,215 --> 00:07:21,952
machines of each type equal that type's time over the cycle, rounded up.

88
00:07:22,352 --> 00:07:28,996
Three people sharing 1.2 minutes of lockstitch work get three machines;

89
00:07:29,016 --> 00:07:33,032
0.2 minutes of bartacking gets one, shared.

90
00:07:33,432 --> 00:07:38,535
The 29-person plan has 20 lockstitch, 8 overlock,

91
00:07:38,555 --> 00:07:46,272
3 bartack and one each of chainstitch, buttonhole and button machines: 34,

92
00:07:46,292 --> 00:07:48,572
about 1.17 per person.

93
00:07:48,972 --> 00:07:56,921
Keep 5 to 10 percent spares for breakdowns and style changes.

94
00:07:58,441 --> 00:08:04,787
With pattern sewers and automatic pocket welters the operator only loads and

95
00:08:04,807 --> 00:08:07,383
unloads while the machine sews,

96
00:08:07,403 --> 00:08:09,979
so one person can tend several.

97
00:08:10,379 --> 00:08:15,679
Let a be load time, w walking time and t sewing time.

98
00:08:16,079 --> 00:08:21,550
Tending n, the operator's round is n times a plus w,

99
00:08:21,570 --> 00:08:27,779
a machine's cycle is a plus t, and the cycle is the larger.

100
00:08:28,179 --> 00:08:35,799
As the lab steps n from 1 to 6, first the operator waits, then the machines do.

101
00:08:36,199 --> 00:08:38,961
Where they're equal is the balance point.

102
00:08:40,481 --> 00:08:53,061
Compute it: a 0.3, w 0.05 and t 0.9 minutes give a balance point of about 3.43.

103
00:08:53,461 --> 00:09:02,648
Tending 3, the cycle is 1.2 and the operator waits 13 percent; tending 4,

104
00:09:02,668 --> 00:09:09,081
the cycle is 1.4 and each machine waits 14 percent.

105
00:09:09,481 --> 00:09:20,645
By cost, at 0.5 yuan wages and 0.08 per machine-minute,

106
00:09:20,665 --> 00:09:28,373
3 cost 0.296 and 4 cost 0.287 a piece.

107
00:09:28,773 --> 00:09:35,538
Make the machine faster, t at 0.6, and the cheapest becomes 3.

108
00:09:37,058 --> 00:09:40,678
Now the special machines.

109
00:09:41,078 --> 00:09:50,925
An automatic pocket welter cuts the back pocket from 1.20 to 0.45 minutes,

110
00:09:50,945 --> 00:09:57,858
a waistband machine the waistband from 1.30 to 0.80.

111
00:09:58,258 --> 00:10:07,303
Work content falls to 11.95 minutes and 26 people make 120 an hour at 91.9

112
00:10:07,323 --> 00:10:09,263
percent balance,

113
00:10:09,283 --> 00:10:14,162
three fewer than adding people up to 29.

114
00:10:14,562 --> 00:10:22,528
But with frequent style changes or no back pockets, the sums don't work.

115
00:10:24,048 --> 00:10:27,920
Robot machine tending is the same model:

116
00:10:27,940 --> 00:10:34,732
the robot loads in time a and moves in w, the machine tool cuts for t,

117
00:10:34,752 --> 00:10:40,668
and the cycle is the larger of n times a plus w and a plus t.

118
00:10:41,068 --> 00:10:44,932
Robots are fast, so one tends more machines;

119
00:10:44,952 --> 00:10:51,466
speed up the machine tools and it tends fewer, so dual fixtures shorten a,

120
00:10:51,486 --> 00:10:55,968
just like alternating templates on a pattern sewer.

121
00:10:56,368 --> 00:11:01,708
Robot lines need balancing too: the slowest cell sets output.

122
00:11:03,228 --> 00:11:05,748
You tend several machines at home too.

123
00:11:06,148 --> 00:11:08,844
On a weekend you put rice in the cooker,

124
00:11:08,864 --> 00:11:12,848
clothes in the washer and bread in the oven, go round once,

125
00:11:12,868 --> 00:11:17,328
and the first one is just finishing: that's multi-machine tending.

126
00:11:17,728 --> 00:11:25,328
Loading the rice or the clothes is a, walking between kitchen and balcony is w,

127
00:11:25,348 --> 00:11:30,053
and the time the machines work on their own is t.

128
00:11:30,453 --> 00:11:35,327
Make all the machines faster and you end up running between them more,

129
00:11:35,347 --> 00:11:37,913
and you can look after fewer at once.

130
00:11:39,433 --> 00:11:41,274
Back to our problem.

131
00:11:41,674 --> 00:11:52,599
Today the cycle is 0.70 minutes: about 85.7 an hour at 72.5 percent balance.

132
00:11:52,999 --> 00:11:58,339
In theory 120 needs at least 27.

133
00:11:58,739 --> 00:12:09,409
Regroup the same 26 and the cycle is 0.55: about 109 an hour at 92.3 percent,

134
00:12:09,429 --> 00:12:17,599
about 27 percent more; with today's equipment 120 needs 29.

135
00:12:17,999 --> 00:12:24,925
With the special machines 26 are enough, saving 18000 yuan a month,

136
00:12:24,945 --> 00:12:28,139
paying back in about 11 months.

137
00:12:29,659 --> 00:12:38,082
Now Virtual lab 28-1, line balancing, which you can do yourself on WenQuest.

138
00:12:38,102 --> 00:12:42,859
Results and tasks are blurred. On page one,

139
00:12:42,879 --> 00:12:49,303
the balance chart and the operation table are on the left;

140
00:12:49,323 --> 00:12:54,747
on the right you set target output and equipment.

141
00:12:54,767 --> 00:13:02,079
First click 'one operation per group' and see which stations wait.

142
00:13:02,479 --> 00:13:09,445
Then click 'automatic grouping' and the bars even out. Record the headcount,

143
00:13:09,465 --> 00:13:13,029
cycle, output and balance, and compare.

144
00:13:13,429 --> 00:13:20,387
Task one: reorder operations with the arrows in the table. 'Make belt loops',

145
00:13:20,407 --> 00:13:25,734
outlined here, has no predecessors. Don't break precedence;

146
00:13:25,754 --> 00:13:30,809
re-cut the groups and see whether you can save a person.

147
00:13:31,209 --> 00:13:34,656
Task two sets the target to 100 an hour,

148
00:13:34,676 --> 00:13:41,071
and task three adds the automatic pocket welter and the waistband machine.

149
00:13:41,091 --> 00:13:46,618
In both, group automatically first, then reorder to save people.

150
00:13:47,018 --> 00:13:50,839
On page two, step the machines tended from 1 to 6,

151
00:13:50,859 --> 00:13:55,064
watch who waits for whom in the operator-machine chart,

152
00:13:55,084 --> 00:14:00,134
record the cycle and cost per piece, and find the cheapest number.

153
00:14:00,154 --> 00:14:05,358
Then cut the automatic sewing time to 0.6 minutes and find it again.

154
00:14:05,758 --> 00:14:13,156
Last, the machine time for one seam: 333 stitches at a top speed of 4000,

155
00:14:13,176 --> 00:14:17,933
5000 and 6000, then 40 stitches for comparison.

156
